  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 233 AM EDT Tue Jul 28 2026\n\nA surface trough is pivoting through our area currently, sparking a \nfew widely scattered storms across northern Indiana and northwest \nOhio. Any stronger storms may produce small hail and gusty winds, \nalthough they are expected to stay below severe limits. This \nactivity is expected to push off to the southeast later this \nmorning, bringing precipitation chances to an end. A trailing cold \nfront is currently positioned from Milwaukee, WI to Muskegon, MI to \nAlpena, MI and is moving slowly south. This cold front will move \nthrough our area later this morning from 12-18Z. Before the front \ngets here, I would not be surprised if patchy dense fog attempts to \ndevelop from 09-13Z this morning. Surface observations do not show \nany fog yet but we will continue to monitor through the overnight \nhours. Surface conditions are decently favorable for fog with the \nsaturated ground from yesterday's afternoon and evening storms. \nWinds are still a bit elevated at 5 to 10 mph out of the south and \nwill eventually switch to be coming from the west closer to \ndaybreak. \n\nDry air, low humidity, sunshine, and cooler temperatures are \nexpected behind the front. Behind the frontal passage, winds will \nswitch again to be coming from the north; a strong CAA push will \nallow for high temperatures to be below normal today only topping \nout in the upper 70s to low 80s. Locations closer to Lake Michigan \nin northwest Indiana and southwest Lower Michigan may not even get \nout of the mid 70s today. The HRRR smoke model depicts wildfire \nsmoke moving in aloft today from active wildfires burning in the \nPacific Northwest. Have adjusted sky cover up to 35% through \nWednesday morning to account for hazy skies. No impacts to surface \nair quality or visibilities are expected.\n\nSeveral more seasonable and dry days are expected Wednesday and \nTuesday as high pressure builds into the Great Lakes region midweek. \nExpect highs to gradually warm by a few degrees each day through \nFriday as the airmass moderates. It will be dry at least through \nThursday; an incoming frontal system over the central Plains will \nmove into the mid Mississippi River valley by Saturday, with \nintermittent low chances (20-30%) for rain/storms returning to our \nforecast area by late Friday and into the weekend. \n\nAlthough it may look like a great day to head out to Lake Michigan \nbeaches today, as winds shift to become northerly, dangerous \nswimming conditions are expected to develop for southeast Lake \nMichigan beaches on Tuesday. Buoys already show waves of 2 to 3 feet \noffshore of La Porte County, IN and Berrien County, MI. Waves will \nreach 3 to 5 feet Tuesday morning and will continue to build to 5 to \n8 feet by this afternoon and evening following the cold frontal \npassage. With direct onshore flow, strong structural, rip, and \nlongshore currents are expected. Structural currents will be \nespecially strong on the north side of piers. Stay out of the water \nand stay off of the piers! Beach Hazards Statements are in effect \nfrom now through daybreak Wednesday for La Porte County, IN and \nBerrien County, MI. A moderate swim risk is ahead for Wednesday with \nwaves of 2 to 3 feet, especially in the morning. Waves are \nanticipated to diminish during the day and a welcome return to much \ncalmer conditions is ahead for Thursday.",
